<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">House of Plantagenet</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Plantagenets, Plantagenet dynasty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The House of Plantagenet was a royal dynasty descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geoffrey-plantagenet-count-of-anjou">Geoffrey Plantagenet, Count of Anjou</a>, whose son became King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II of England</a> in 1154. Through inheritance and marriage Henry ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, Normandy, Anjou and Aquitaine, the so-called Angevin Empire, though his son John lost Normandy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1204. The dynasty and its cadet branches, Lancaster and York, held the English throne until 1485.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/house-of-plantagenet.jpg" alt="House of Plantagenet" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blason_maison_Plantagen%C3%AAt.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The house originated in the French county of Anjou. The later counts of Anjou descended from Geoffrey II, Count of Gâtinais, and his wife Ermengarde of Anjou, who in 1060 inherited the title through cognatic kinship from an Angevin family descended from a noble named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingelger">Ingelger</a>, whose recorded history dates from 870. Plantegenest was a twelfth-century nickname of Geoffrey, Count of Anjou and Duke of Normandy. One theory derives it from the blossom of the common broom, called genista in medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. In the fifteenth century Richard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, 3rd Duke of York, adopted Plantagenet as his family name.</p><p>Henry II was born in 1133, and his grandfather Henry I is reported to have been delighted, calling the boy the heir to the kingdom. The first three Plantagenet kings, Henry II, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> and John, ruled extensive territories in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and are sometimes called the Angevins, though some historians use that term for the whole dynasty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s marriage to Eleanor, who had already had two daughters by her first husband, King Louis, produced eight children in thirteen years. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> left no heir, a succession crisis arose between supporters of his nephew Arthur and of John. John won a significant victory at the Battle of Mirebeau, but he lost Anjou and most of his other French territories, which made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the centre of the realm. His son Henry III, king for most of the thirteenth century, faced baronial conflict that helped establish Parliament, and the kings were often forced to accept compromises such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-carta">Magna Carta</a>.</p><p>Edward I enforced his claims in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statute-of-rhuddlan">Statute of Rhuddlan</a> established <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>'s authority over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. In 1328 Charles IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> died without a male heir, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-vi-of-france">Philip of Valois</a> became king over Queen Isabella's claim on behalf of her son Edward III. By the end of Edward III's reign the dynasty had adopted Middle English as the language of governance. Richard II, who came to the throne as a boy, faced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peasants-revolt">Peasants' Revolt</a> of 1381 after poll taxes were levied to pay for military campaigns.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the deposition of Richard II in 1399, two cadet branches, Lancaster and York, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Henry V won a near total victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt">Agincourt</a> and secured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-troyes">Treaty of Troyes</a>, but his son Henry VI, who succeeded at nine months old, lost the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. The rivalry of Lancaster and York brought about the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a>, which ended at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bosworth-field">Battle of Bosworth Field</a> in 1485, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-iii-of-england">Richard III</a> died. Henry VII, a Lancastrian through his mother, then married Elizabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-tudor">Tudor dynasty</a> began.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “House of Plantagenet” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1066 the Norman kings ruled through castle-building and feudal knight service, and their <b>Plantagenet</b> successors fought on several fronts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A poor upland county of pasture and woodland, La Marche mattered for its strategic place between the <b>Plantagenet</b> and Capetian spheres.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-la-marche">County of La Marche</a></li><li><blockquote>The viscounts were middling lords caught between Aquitaine, the <b>Plantagenet</b> kings and the French crown, often clashing with their own townsmen and with English overlords; Richard the Lionheart died in 1199 besieging Châlus, a castle in the viscounty.</blockquote><a href="/state/viscounty-of-limoges">Viscounty of Limoges</a></li></ul></section>
